What if changing the whole object instead of changing the properties :
            it create a new object does not affect the another one. 


In javascript datas are stored in two different types :
      1)primitive types (values are stored as copy).
      2)Reference Type (Address/ it copy like a reference).

 1)primitive types :
            number, string, boolean, undefined, null, symbol, bigint.
                        Example :
                               let x = 10;
                               let y = x;
                               y = 20;
                               console.log(x, y); // 10 20 — x  value doesnt change!

 2)Reference types
            object, array, function
            this copy the address of the value but not copy the actual memory.
            Example :
               let a = { name: "joshika", age: 20 };
               let b = a;   //  key point
                        Here, b = a means, a's object is not copied to b, it only poit to yhe memory of a.
                   a ----\
                          \--> { name: "joshika", age: 20 }  (same object in memory)
                    b ----/


Const in object :
